1
00:00:02,259 --> 00:00:05,063
Deno has this built-in permissions concept,

2
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:07,884
and that's a key difference to Node, in case

3
00:00:07,884 --> 00:00:08,573
you know Node.

4
00:00:09,150 --> 00:00:12,707
In Node.js, you can execute any script just

5
00:00:12,755 --> 00:00:15,736
like this, and Node will not ask for

6
00:00:15,736 --> 00:00:18,685
permissions. So any script is allowed to do

7
00:00:18,845 --> 00:00:19,758
anything in the end.

8
00:00:20,464 --> 00:00:21,569
That's different in Deno.

9
00:00:22,130 --> 00:00:22,963
By default,

10
00:00:23,508 --> 00:00:27,165
Deno scripts may not read files, write

11
00:00:27,262 --> 00:00:27,727
files,

12
00:00:28,305 --> 00:00:31,035
or use environment variables, or interact

13
00:00:31,035 --> 00:00:31,950
with the network.

14
00:00:32,721 --> 00:00:34,375
And there are a couple of other things too,

15
00:00:34,487 --> 00:00:36,478
but these are the most important things

16
00:00:36,510 --> 00:00:38,565
which are not allowed by default.

17
00:00:39,705 --> 00:00:42,146
Now that's a great security mechanism, but

18
00:00:42,194 --> 00:00:42,820
sometimes

19
00:00:43,479 --> 00:00:44,571
you want to write a file.

20
00:00:45,823 --> 00:00:48,376
For example, here in our code, we want to

21
00:00:48,392 --> 00:00:50,544
run this code. We don't want it to crash.

22
00:00:51,202 --> 00:00:53,900
Well, no worries. Of course, you can still

23
00:00:54,093 --> 00:00:57,193
do these things with Deno. You just have to

24
00:00:57,225 --> 00:00:59,795
assign the right permissions to your script

25
00:00:59,795 --> 00:01:01,449
execution explicitly.

26
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:05,014
And for example, for reading files, that's

27
00:01:05,014 --> 00:01:05,785
done by adding

28
00:01:06,460 --> 00:01:09,383
the --allow-read flag to your Deno run

29
00:01:09,511 --> 00:01:12,820
command. For writing, it's --allow-write.

30
00:01:13,286 --> 00:01:16,803
It's --allow-env for accessing environment

31
00:01:16,803 --> 00:01:19,869
variables. And it's --allow-net for

32
00:01:19,869 --> 00:01:21,490
interacting with the network.

33
00:01:22,357 --> 00:01:25,455
There also are variations in case you don't

34
00:01:25,471 --> 00:01:27,798
wanna allow reading all files but you wanna

35
00:01:27,798 --> 00:01:30,141
restrict it to certain files. You can, for

36
00:01:30,141 --> 00:01:32,950
example, also pass the path to the file

37
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:35,583
which should be allowed to allow-read. The

38
00:01:35,583 --> 00:01:38,135
same for writing. You can make clear which

39
00:01:38,167 --> 00:01:40,638
environment variables should be accessible,

40
00:01:41,056 --> 00:01:43,592
and you can, for example, also lock down

41
00:01:43,624 --> 00:01:45,839
network access to specific domains.

42
00:01:46,417 --> 00:01:49,594
So it's not an either nothing or everything

43
00:01:49,707 --> 00:01:52,580
decision, it's a very granular decision

44
00:01:52,852 --> 00:01:55,131
where you can really fine-tune this to your

45
00:01:55,517 --> 00:01:55,870
needs.

46
00:01:56,961 --> 00:01:57,330
So

47
00:01:57,892 --> 00:01:59,112
if I go back here,

48
00:01:59,818 --> 00:02:02,771
in order to run our code, we need to run

49
00:02:03,044 --> 00:02:04,055
deno run

50
00:02:04,729 --> 00:02:05,002
and then

51
00:02:06,478 --> 00:02:07,249
--allow-write

52
00:02:07,859 --> 00:02:10,395
to allow writing to all files,

53
00:02:11,454 --> 00:02:11,919
or

54
00:02:12,689 --> 00:02:15,718
If we wanna just allow writing to test.txt,

55
00:02:16,423 --> 00:02:19,019
allowWrite equals test.txt

56
00:02:19,724 --> 00:02:22,497
and then your script file name, so

57
00:02:22,609 --> 00:02:23,779
deno-core.ts.

58
00:02:24,372 --> 00:02:27,145
And now this compiles and eventually is

59
00:02:27,337 --> 00:02:29,982
done. And as you see, it created the

60
00:02:29,982 --> 00:02:31,697
test.txt file

61
00:02:32,306 --> 00:02:36,120
with the message we specified in our code.

62
00:02:36,937 --> 00:02:40,031
So that is how Deno works in general and how

63
00:02:40,143 --> 00:02:42,564
you deal with its permissions. But not just

64
00:02:42,564 --> 00:02:46,626
that, now you also saw some of the core APIs

65
00:02:46,803 --> 00:02:47,348
in action.

66
00:02:47,926 --> 00:02:50,399
You saw setTimeout, which is available in

67
00:02:50,495 --> 00:02:53,417
Deno, and this global Deno object,

68
00:02:53,947 --> 00:02:56,724
which my IDE doesn't recognize right now,

69
00:02:57,093 --> 00:02:58,121
but which is available,

70
00:02:58,843 --> 00:03:01,107
as you can clearly tell by the fact that the

71
00:03:01,107 --> 00:03:02,841
script executed successfully.

72
00:03:03,499 --> 00:03:06,389
And this global Deno object gives us access

73
00:03:06,389 --> 00:03:09,536
to all these core APIs which essentially

74
00:03:09,536 --> 00:03:12,124
contain all you might need to build any kind

75
00:03:12,124 --> 00:03:15,387
of application. In this case, a very simple

76
00:03:15,387 --> 00:03:18,489
application that writes to a file and

77
00:03:18,538 --> 00:03:21,206
creates the file if it doesn't exist yet.
